PUT /properties versioning — SERVER-SIDE PATCH, NOT APPLIED (schedule as a coordinated client+server change)
==========================================================================================================

Applies on top of branch claude/billing-hardening (commit e1c94f0) with:
    git am put-versioning.patch        # or: git apply --index put-versioning.patch
Verified: `JWT_SECRET=ci-only-secret node --test` is green with it applied (test/property-versioning.test.mjs added).

What the server does after the patch
------------------------------------
- properties gets `version INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1` (PRAGMA-guarded ALTER, existing rows start at 1).
- GET /properties/:id returns { inspection, version } and an ETag "<version>"; list summaries include version.
- PUT /properties/:id reads If-Match (quoted / weak forms accepted) or body.baseVersion. If the base is
  given and differs from the stored version: 409 { code: "version_conflict", version: <current> }, checked
  BEFORE metering (a refused save consumes no allowance) and again atomically in the upsert's WHERE clause.
  Success returns { ok, updatedAt, version } (+ ETag). No base at all = unconditional write, exactly what
  every save is today (old builds, and the app's offline replay reconcilePendingWrites, keep working).
- The property-count 409 now carries code "property_limit" (+ limit) so it can be told apart from a conflict.
- A malformed base is 400 bad_version.

Why it must NOT ship before the client is changed (home-inspection-assistant src/domain/sessionBackend.ts,
saveUserProperty ~L862-905 and reconcilePendingWrites ~L648-690)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Today the server never sends a 409 for a stale save, so the client's conflict branch is dead code. The
moment the server starts sending version_conflict, the CURRENT client's branch runs, and it:
  1. calls markClean(username, id) on ANY 409/412 — the dirty flag is dropped, so reconcilePendingWrites will
     never push that record again and the "dirty-first" boot guard will fetch the server copy on reload. The
     edits made on the losing device since it loaded the record are silently abandoned (they linger in the
     IndexedDB mirror until the next local save overwrites them, but nothing surfaces or merges them).
  2. does not distinguish codes: the property-cap 409 is also treated as a conflict (already a bug today).
  3. keeps versions only in an in-memory Map per tab (propertyVersions) and stamps the next version as
     data.version ?? baseVersion + 1. reconcilePendingWrites sends NO base and never stamps the version it
     gets back, so after an offline replay the still-open tab holds an old number: its next autosave is a
     SELF-conflict → 409 → its own edits are marked clean and dropped even though this device was the last
     writer. Two tabs on one device (shared IndexedDB, separate Maps) produce the same self-conflict on
     every autosave.
So with the server patch alone, every real or spurious conflict makes a device discard its local work with
a "reload" message; today the same situation silently overwrites the other device's work instead. Both
lose data — the patch only changes WHO loses. It is only a net gain once the client is changed to:
  a. enter the conflict branch ONLY when body.code === "version_conflict" (and surface data.error otherwise,
     leaving the record dirty — the property_limit case),
  b. on a version_conflict keep the local copy AND the dirty flag, and offer "keep mine (overwrite) / take
     the server's / reload" instead of markClean,
  c. stamp propertyVersions from EVERY successful PUT and GET, including reconcilePendingWrites, and persist
     the base version next to the dirty set (e.g. hia:dirty:v1 as { id: version }) so a reload or a second
     tab starts from the right number,
  d. send baseVersion from reconcilePendingWrites too (or deliberately leave it unconditional and document
     that offline replays are last-writer-wins).
Deploy order: client build with a–d first (it tolerates a server that returns no version: data.version is
optional), then this server patch.

Rollback: the version column is additive; reverting the server code leaves it unused.

---------------------------------------------------------------- patch follows (git am / git apply --index) ----

